From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 09:44:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Parse external command output through a binary-declared lens

Compiled binaries can carry linking metadata: a small versioned JSON blob
({"v":1,"stdout":{"lens":"json"}}) embedded at link time in a dedicated
ELF section (.ix.link) naming the lens -- a shell-owned parsing
strategy -- that applies to the binary's stdout. Today users hand-write
`^tool | from json` or keep per-tool wrappers; with the section present,
run-external reads the declaration without executing anything and applies
the lens automatically, so a bare `^tool` comes back structured.

Reading is best-effort: a missing section, an unreadable or non-ELF
binary, a malformed blob, or an unknown lens name leaves behavior exactly
as it is today. Output merged with stderr (o+e>|) or redirected to a file
or the null device is never lensed, since raw bytes are the point there.
Only the "json" lens is implemented; the schema is versioned so more
metadata (stderr format, completions, ...) can ride the same section
later.

The producer side (a Rust macro embedding the blob) lives in
indexable-inc/index under packages/link-meta.

